e line can throw a TypeError from interpolating raw.schema when raw is null, rather than the intended condition-based \"unsupported schema\" Error. Actually the errata phrasing \"not by a `&&` condition\" — meaning the TypeError is not caused by the `&&` condition itself but by the template literal interpolation. That's accurate: the `&&` condition correctly evaluates to true (via `!raw`), but the `throw` expression evaluates the template literal which accesses `raw.schema` on null → TypeError.\n\nAlso note operator precedence: `raw.schema && raw.schema !== SCHEMA` — `!==` binds tighter than `&&`, so it's `raw.schema && (raw.schema !== SCHEMA)`. Fine.\n\nAnd the throws at lines 19-21: line 19 `throw new Error(...)`, line 20 `throw new Error('providers is required')`, line 21 `throw new Error('models is required')` — none set `error.code`. Only the catch at line 15-17 sets `error.code = 'E_CONFIG'`. So yes, errata (a) is CONFIRMED.\n\nlib/select.mjs forecast call sites:\n- line 94: `const f = forecast(...)` inside candidates.filter — yes\n- line 102: `const f = forecast(...)` inside `rank` function, which is called from the sort comparator (line 110). So :102 is inside rank(), used by sort comparator. The errata says \":102 inside a sort comparator\". Strictly, it's inside `rank`, which is called by the comparator. Close enough — conceptually the forecast runs within sorting. Let me be precise: it's inside `rank()` which is invoked by `candidates.sort((a,b)=>...)`. So the forecast executes during sort. The claim is essentially correct.\n- line 116: `const f = forecast(...)` inside picked.map — yes.\n\nSo THREE forecast call sites at :94, :102, :116. CONFIRMED. And :102 is in the ranking function used by the sort comparator.\n\nNow I need to check the existence of:\n- `run --lease`\n- `cli resolve`\n- wrapped `call`\n\nThe redesign claims they do NOT exist yet. I need to verify. Let me read lib/cli.mjs, bin/cliworker.mjs, lib/index.js, and grep for \"lease\", \"resolv