output shows line \"2→\" with nothing, meaning there's a line 2 that's empty, i.e., the file has a trailing newline. Actually the content is all on line 1.\n\nWait, actually look again: the Read output shows:\n```\n   1→【1 ModelRequest v1】... [truncated]\n   2→\n```\nSo line 1 is the huge content (truncated at 2000 chars), and line 2 is empty. So the whole spec is one line.\n\nI need section headings 第 1 节 through 第 15 节. These are marked as 【1 ...】, 【2 ...】, etc. in the text (Chinese brackets 【N ...】). The redesign cites \"interfaces 第 1～15 节\", \"第 6～8 节\", \"第 11 节\", \"第 2 节\".\n\nSo the file uses 【N Title】 markers. I need to list all of them. To do that with Grep, I need to extract all 【N ...】 occurrences. \n\nSince Grep can't do \"only matching,\" maybe I can use a trick: use a regex that matches the whole line but... no.\n\nAlternative: Try PowerShell tool. Let me try a read-only PowerShell command. Actually, let me first check whether PowerShell is permitted by trying a harmless read-only command. If it's denied, I'll fall back.\n\nActually, let me reconsider — maybe there IS a way with Bash. The denial message said permission prompts not available in non-interactive mode. Perhaps certain commands are allowed. But `ls -la` was denied. Let me not retry Bash maliciously. However, trying a different read-only command isn't \"malicious workaround\" — but the denial applies to the Bash tool generally, not specific commands. Retrying would likely be denied again. I'll avoid it.\n\nLet me try PowerShell for reading the file content. It's a legitimate read-only operation needed to answer the question. Let me try.\n\nActually, let me think about whether I even need the full content. The user wants: \"give its path and list its section headings (第 1 节 ... 第 15 节)\". So I need the 15 section headings. I need to extract them.\n\nLet me try PowerShell with a command that reads the file and uses regex to find 【\\d+ ...】 patterns, outputting each match. This is read-only.\n\nAlternatively, Grep with pattern `【1[0-5]?】|【[1-9]】` ... but again full-line output.\n\nHold on — maybe Grep's output isn't actually omitted if I use a tighter approach. The message \"[Omitted long matching line]\" came because the matched line is > some threshold. But if I could match and the tool had \"only matching\"... \n\nLet me try a different approach with Grep: use `-o`? Not available.\n\nOK here's another idea: The Grep tool uses ripgrep. ripgrep supports `--only-matching` via `-o`. The tool doesn't expose it. But maybe I can pass it inside the pattern? No.\n\nLet me just try PowerShell. Actually, wait — let me reconsider. I should also try the Bash denial again? No.\n\nLet me try PowerShell tool.\n\nActually, I realize I should think about what's allowed. The plan mode says only read-only tools. PowerShell reading a file is read-only. Let me try it.\n\nBut also, I want to be efficient. Let me do a combined approach:\n1. PowerShell to extract section headings and full content of interfaces.md.\n2. Glob to check existence of root-level directories/files (contracts/, test/traceability/, test/flows/steps/, test/characterization/, scripts/ci-matrix.json, .github/workflows/ci.yml).\n3. Grep for B.4.4, 金标, review-v1, etc. across all files including json.\n4. Read the .collab files for one-line descriptions.\n\nNote: My earlier Grep for \"B.4.4|金标|review-v1|registry.snapshot|contracts/\" returned \"No matches found\" — but that Grep might have only searched certain file types by default? No, Grep searches all files. But wait, it said no matches, yet there's a file `review-v1.verdict.json` in .collab! That means the Grep didn't find content containing \"review-v1\" as text — the filename has it but not content. And \"B.4.4\" and \"金标\" and \"registry.snapshot\" and \"contracts/\" — no matches. Interesting. But I should double-check by searching more broadly, including hidden files. Grep might ignore hidden directories like `.collab`? Actually the earlier Grep th