{"type":"assistant","uuid":"01a1b5c6-78ad-7307-b3e2-e83f4f56cade","session_id":"01a1b5bf-5519-7a7b-8d8d-80d1042af7bf","message":{"id":"01a1b5c678ad7307b3e2e83da680de8e","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have very solid evidence. Key findings:\n\n1. `interfaces.md` exists at `.collab/blueprint/cliworker-v2/interfaces.md`. It's a single-line file with 【N】 markers. Section 2 contains E_INSUFFICIENT_QUOTA/E_NO_CANDIDATE rules. The section citations in task-graph.json (T-004 inputs \"interfaces 第 1～15 节\", T-005 \"interfaces 第 5～7 节\", T-006 \"interfaces 第 6、11 节\", AC-016 \"resolvePaths 规则按 interfaces 第 11 节\", AC-xxx \"按 interfaces 第 2 节\") all reference this real file. So the \"interfaces spec\" is a REAL committed artifact.\n\nBut I still couldn't extract all 15 section headings verbatim because the file is one ~10k-char line and Read truncates at 2000 chars. I have sections 1-3 verbatim, and I know from cross-references the topics of 2, 5, 6-8, 10, 11. Should I keep trying? Let me think about whether there's a way.\n\nAH WAIT — I just realized something! The designer.log contains the designer's STREAM output. The interfaces.md was likely WRITTEN by the designer as a tool call with the full content. If the content was written with \\n escapes in JSON, then in the log it appears as a long line with \"\\n\" literal sequences. But if the log records the Write tool call's `content` with actual newlines escaped as `\\n`, then reading that line would show the escaped newlines as `\\n` text — and I could grep for `【4` within that long line... still omitted.\n\nHmm. But — hold on! The interfaces.md is a SINGLE line in the file. Why? Because the requirement doc said \"正文里禁止出现 ASCII 双引号\" and \"所有 JSON 接口示例都放进最后那个 JSON 对象的 interfaces 字段里\" — the designer's output was a JSON object with an `interfaces` field containing the spec. When written to interfaces.md, the whole spec (with \\n as literal escape or actual newlines?) — hmm, the file appears as one line, so likely the designer put the entire spec as a single string without real newlines (using 【N】 instead of \\n).\n\nSo the interfaces.md file is genuinely one line. There's no way to get around the 2000-char truncation with my allowed tools (Glob/Grep/Read only; Bash/PowerShell denied).\n\nBUT — maybe the designer.log has the FULL spec content in a line that I could read via a different technique. Hmm, all the log lines are also long and truncated by Read.\n\nHmm, wait — actually, could I use Grep to find the headings in the designer.log with a SHORT line? The designer.log STREAM lines are JSON objects (long lines). But the PROMPT section (lines 1-91) is multi-line readable. The full spec would be in a STREAM line (long). So no.\n\nHmm, actually — maybe there IS a chance: the designer.log line 438/439/970/971 had 【N markers on long lines. But maybe OTHER lines in designer.log contain the spec with actual newlines preserved (if the log writer wrote the tool input content with real \\n). If the log has real newlines inside a JSON string... no, JSON strings can't have raw newlines.\n\nOK. Let me make a FINAL decision: I'll be honest and report:\n- interfaces.md exists (real committed artifact, path given).\n- It uses 【1】...【15】 section markers (I'll confirm count via a targeted grep). Actually let me confirm the max section number with a couple of greps: `【15` and `【10` etc.\n\nActually, I can confirm 15 sections efficiently: use Grep `output_mode: \"count\"` won't help. But `files_with_matches` with patterns for each section: I can do ONE Grep with a regex that requires sections 1 through 15 in sequence on the same line: `【1 [^】]*】.*【2 [^】]*】.*...` — but that's long. Simpler: Grep `【15` with output_mode \"content\" → if \"[Omitted long matching line]\" appears, section 15 exists. And `【14`, `【13`, etc. Actually a single pattern `【1[0-5]` would match any of 10-15; but to confirm 15 specifically, grep `【15 ` or `【15[^】]*】`.\n\nLet me do a small set of targeted greps to confir